       }
      ],
      "text": "Regions:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " cervical (7), thoracic (12), lumbar (5), sacrum (5 fused), coccyx (3–4 fused)."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "C1 and C2:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " C1 (atlas) has no body; C2 (axis) carries the odontoid process (dens)."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Surface landmarks:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " the C7 spinous process (vertebra prominens) at the base of the neck; spine of the scapula at about T3; PSIS dimples at about S2; inferior angle of the scapula at about T7."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Tuffier's line:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " the highest points of the iliac crests are at about L4, used to find the L3–L4 or L4–L5 space for lumbar puncture."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Deep intrinsic back muscles:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " erector spinae (iliocostalis, longissimus, spinalis) and transversospinalis (semispinalis, multifidus, rotatores)."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Ligaments:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " the anterior longitudinal ligament (front of the bodies) limits extension; the posterior longitudinal ligament (back of the bodies, inside the canal) limits flexion; the ligamentum flavum joins adjacent laminae, and its hypertrophy causes spinal stenosis."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Discs:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " the nucleus pulposus absorbs and distributes axial load; the annulus fibrosus resists tension and torsion."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Disc aging:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " the nucleus dehydrates and loses height (degenerative disc disease); annular tears let the nucleus herniate and compress a nerve root."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Spinal nerves:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " 31 pairs (8 cervical, 12 thoracic, 5 lumbar, 5 sacral, 1 coccygeal), each with a dorsal (sensory) and ventral (motor) root."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Plexuses:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " cervical C1–C4 (phrenic nerve from C3–C5); brachial C5–T1 (upper limb); lumbar L1–L4 (femoral and obturator); sacral L4–S4 (sciatic, the largest nerve, dividing into tibial and common fibular)."
     }
    ]
   }
